<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · island</p><h1 class="enc-title">Euboea</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Evia, Negroponte</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Euboea is the second-largest Greek island, lying along the east coast of central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a> and separated from the mainland by the narrow Euripus strait. Its cities of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chalcis">Chalcis</a> and Eretria led early Greek colonisation in the 8th century BCE, founding settlements in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a> and the northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aegean">Aegean</a>. Under the Venetians and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> lords it was known as Negroponte, until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> took it in 1470.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/euboea.jpg" alt="Euboea" width="320" height="398" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: NASA · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Euboea_from_space.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Under the Villehardouins the lords of Athens and <b>Euboea</b>, the Duke of the Archipelago and the Marquis of Bodonitsa recognised the Prince of Achaea as their suzerain.</blockquote><a href="/empire/principality-of-achaea">Principality of Achaea · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>The advance of the Ottoman Empire cost it <b>Negroponte</b> in 1470, Modon and Coron in 1500, Cyprus in 1571 and Crete in 1669, while the opening of the Portuguese sea route to India after 1498 and the later rise of Dutch and English shipping eroded its position in…</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-venice">Republic of Venice · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Sultan Mehmed II personally led the Ottoman siege of <b>Negroponte</b> (Chalcis), capital of the Venetian possession of Euboea.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/july-12">12 July in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Andros, just south-east of <b>Euboea</b>, was settled by Ionian Greeks in the early Iron Age.</blockquote><a href="/state/andros">Andros</a></li><li><blockquote>Chalcis grew on the narrowest point of the Euripus channel between <b>Euboea</b> and mainland Greece.</blockquote><a href="/state/chalcis">Chalcis</a></li><li><blockquote>Eretria grew on the south coast of <b>Euboea</b>, facing Attica, in the 8th century BCE, possibly succeeding the earlier settlement at Lefkandi.</blockquote><a href="/state/eretria">Eretria</a></li></ul></section>
